Confirmed by code reading. I did not re-probe it; the implementer reproduced it at 2ecbf57.

The request's lock order:
- it holds the job row FOR SHARE from helper :45 until commit;
- private.request_scoped_automatic_quote_impl (20260812044000) then upserts the job's service_request_line_items row (`on conflict (job_id, service_type, scope) do update`);
- it then runs `update public.jobs set status = 'quoting'`.

The cancel's lock order (api_cancel_quote_request, 20261002182714, authenticated):
- `update public.quote_requests set status='canceled'` fires sync_service_request_line_item_status_on_quote_request (20260331010000:62-65), which updates the same line item;
- it then runs `update public.jobs`, which waits on the request's FOR SHARE.

The base had no request job-row lock before the line item, so there was no cycle there. The PR body's account and its unticked P1 box are accurate. The migration header at :24-27 ("cannot close a lock cycle through the request's scope row locks") is literally about reversed-order scope writers, but it reads broader than the truth, since P1 waits on the request's job-row scope lock.

The fix options all change the decided "same rows, same strength" lock set:
- NOWAIT on the line item;
- FOR NO KEY UPDATE NOWAIT on the job row;
- reorder the cancel.

